  "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: from 1877 to 1973 the Department stated a short test for recognizing a Latin American regime that took power by force (actual control, assent or absence of resistance, and ability and willingness to meet international obligations), added at times a constitutional test and treaty-based bars, and asked specific things first: pledges to pay claims and borrow on American terms (Nicaragua 1911), a property treaty (Mexico 1921), elections (Bolivia 1920, Peru 1962), cooperation against the Axis (Argentina 1944), civil liberties and observers (Peru 1962) and private anti-Communist pledges (Bolivia 1964); from 1948 it said non-recognition was a poor lever. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed papers came almost entirely from the State Department central decimal file (Record Group 59), not further resolved offline, plus pre-1906 despatch and instruction rolls and one Policy Planning Staff lot (64 D 563); the footnotes cite the same decimal file (70 citations in 60 documents) and a handful of lots, listed in the report. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the Latin American side, anything after 1980 in this library, the 1861–1894 volumes (sampled, not read), and whether pledges were kept.",
